{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The path down from the headland was narrower than Mira remembered, lined with sea thrift and pale grasses that bent without any wind. The man from the photograph walked ahead, his coat collar turned up out of old habit, though the night was neither cold nor warm. Tobias kept her hand in his, and every so often he squeezed it, as if to make sure she was still there. At the bottom of the cliff the village waited, or what had once been the village. The cottages stood in their familiar crooked row, whitewashed walls glowing faintly under the stars, but the windows held no lamps. Instead, each doorway was open, and from inside came the murmur of voices, the clink of cups, a fiddle tuning somewhere at the far end of the lane. \"They've been expecting you,\" the man said. Mira recognized the Hallorans' door, painted that stubborn blue Agnes had insisted on for forty years. She recognized the bench outside the chandlery where the old fishermen used to argue about the weather. A child ran past them, barefoot, laughing, and did not leave footprints in the sand between the stones. Tobias stopped at the gate of the house they had lived in before the lighthouse, before the long winters and the endless stairs. Roses climbed the fence, blooming out of season. \"Do you think we can stay?\" he asked quietly. Mira looked out at the harbor, where boats rocked gently at their moorings, sails furled, lanterns lit, each one safely home. \"I think,\" she said, \"we already have.\""}
